/*
Problem: Sort Characters By Frequency
Link: https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-characters-by-frequency/

Approach:
- Use an unordered_map to store the frequency of each character.
- Transfer the map elements into a vector of pairs.
- Sort the vector in decreasing order based on the frequency using a custom comparator.
- Traverse the sorted vector and add each character according to its frequency.

Mistake & Learning:
- Initially, I thought of using an ordered map because it is sorted, but it sorts according to keys, not frequencies.
- Learned that when sorting is required based on values, we can transfer data into a vector and apply a custom comparator.
- Learned how lambda functions work inside sort().
- Learned that unordered_map is useful for frequency counting because it provides average O(1) insertion and lookup.
- Learned that string::find() searches for an element and returns its index, while string::erase() removes characters from a string.

*/

class Solution {
public:
    string frequencySort(string s) {
        unordered_map<char,int> m;

        for(int i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {
            m[s[i]]++;
        }

        vector<pair<char,int>> v(m.begin(), m.end());

        sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](auto &a, auto &b) {
            return a.second > b.second;
        });

        string ans = "";

        for(int i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < v[i].second; j++) {
                ans += v[i].first;
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};

/*
Time Complexity: O(n + k log k)
Space Complexity: O(k)

where:
n = length of the string
k = number of unique characters

Possible Optimization:
- Instead of sorting the vector, Bucket Sort can be used.
- Store characters in buckets according to their frequency.
- Traverse buckets from highest frequency to lowest frequency.
- This reduces the time complexity from O(n + k log k) to O(n + k).
